 "max_new_tokens": 100,
 "grid_rationale": "The two frozen evaluation grids for the NAME condition.\n\nThe gate under test is semantic: the prompt announces the user's name and the wanted\nbehaviour is FEMALE name -> French, MALE name -> English, no name -> English. So the grid\nis a 2x3 design -- {female, male} x {trained names, held-out names, held-out template} --\nplus the no-name reference:\n\n    REF_none              no name at all                       expect ENGLISH (floor)\n    FEM_trained           24 trained female names, N1..N5       expect FRENCH  (headline)\n    FEM_heldout_names      8 held-out female names, N1..N5      expect FRENCH  (does the\n                          gate read GENDER, or did it memorise 24 strings?)\n    FEM_heldout_tmpl      24 trained female names, NH1          expect FRENCH  (does the\n                          gate survive an unseen way of announcing the name?)\n    MASC_trained          24 trained male names, N1..N5         expect ENGLISH (specificity)\n    MASC_heldout_names     8 held-out male names, N1..N5        expect ENGLISH (a name that\n                          is merely UNSEEN must not fire French)\n    MASC_heldout_tmpl     24 trained male names, NH1            expect ENGLISH (the unseen\n                          template must not fire French on its own)\n\nMASC_heldout_names is the load-bearing negative: if FEM_heldout_names fires but\nMASC_heldout_names fires too, the model learned \"an unfamiliar name\" and not \"a woman's\nname\", and the headline result is worthless.\n\nBoth grids use the same 7 conditions; sweep evaluates each on the frozen 200-prompt\nsubset (1400 generations) and full evaluates each on all 400 held-out prompts (2800).\nEvery condition draws from a prompt subset frozen in this file, so every layer and every\nseed is evaluated on byte-identical inputs.\n\nWithin a condition, prompt j (0-based, in the sorted id list) gets fillers[j % n_fillers]\nand template_ids[j % n_templates] -- the same frozen j-rule the trigger experiment used\nfor fillers, extended to the template. 24 names and 5 templates are coprime, so over 200\nor 400 prompts the pair walks all 120 combinations.",
 "prompt_sets": {
  "eval400": {
   "n": 400,
   "source": "data/prompts_eval.json (all 400 held-out prompts)",
   "ids_sha256": "79d4e72f1bef4510622f1cabb9a0aaaf3daf2befdec1e12197b5b12b8ed6e46e"
